    gettext_noop("Some values coming from untrusted sources are evaluated, so a string like <code>${...}</code> in these values is replaced by the evaluated expression, for example secured data (<code>${sec.data.xxx}</code>), an environment variable (<code>${env:XXX}</code>) or the result of other expressions.\n<br>\nThis happens in the following cases:\n<ul>\n  <li>Channel names saved automatically in server option <code>autojoin</code> when the option <code>autojoin_dynamic</code> is enabled (WeeChat ≥ 3.5): a channel name may come from the server, for example a channel forward with numeric 470; the channel name is evaluated when the server option <code>autojoin</code> is used.</li>\n  <li>Server options <code>addresses</code>, <code>nicks</code>, <code>password</code> and <code>autojoin</code> set from an IRC URL (<code>irc://...</code>) given on command line or with command <code>/connect</code> (WeeChat ≥ 0.4.2): the URL may be untrusted, for example when WeeChat is the handler of <code>irc://</code> links.</li>\n  <li>Variables <code>$nick</code>, <code>$channel</code> and <code>$server</code> in commands <code>/allchan</code>, <code>/allpv</code> and <code>/allserv</code> (WeeChat ≥ 3.0): they are replaced before the command is evaluated, and nick and channel names come from the server.</li>\n</ul>\nA malicious IRC server, or any user able to forward a channel or to send a private message, could disclose secured data (for example passwords) or environment variables, sent back to the server in a JOIN or another command, or cause high CPU and memory usage with expressions like <code>${repeat:...}</code>.")
    gettext_noop("Stack buffer overflow when building a JOIN command with channel keys.")
    gettext_noop("Stack-based Buffer Overflow")
    gettext_noop("Strings are built with uncontrolled format in API function infobar_printf.")
    gettext_noop("Strings are built with uncontrolled format when IRC commands are redirected by relay plugin. If the output or redirected command contains formatting chars like \"%\", this can lead to a crash of WeeChat.")
    gettext_noop("Strings are built with uncontrolled format when nicks containing \"%\" are monitored with command <code>/notify</code>.")
    gettext_noop("Strings are built with uncontrolled format when unknown IRC commands are sent to server, if option <code>irc.network.send_unknown_commands</code> is enabled.")
    gettext_noop("The messages received on \"handshake\" endpoint, which does not require authentication, are not freed if the content is not a JSON object (for example an array or any other JSON type).\n<br>\nA malicious client can send large arrays and cause memory exhaustion. The WeeChat process can be killed by the OOM killer.")
    gettext_noop("The option <code>xfer.file.auto_accept_nicks</code> is a list of nicks for which incoming files and chats are automatically accepted, with the format <code>server.nick</code> (for a specific server) or <code>nick</code> (for all servers).\n<br>\nFor the format <code>server.nick</code>, only the beginning of the server name is compared, so the nick is also auto-accepted on any other server whose name starts with the same chars.\n<br>\nFor example with <code>libera.alice</code>, files and chats sent by nick <code>alice</code> are automatically accepted on servers <code>libera</code>, <code>libera2</code> or <code>libera_test</code>.\n<br>\nOn these other servers, any user able to use this nick (which may not be registered there) can send files that are automatically saved in the download directory, or start DCC chats, without confirmation.")
    gettext_noop("The output buffer used for charset conversion was allocated with 4 bytes per input byte, but some charsets like <code>TSCII</code> produce more (up to 12 bytes), and the replacement char \"?\" for invalid chars was written without checking the remaining size, so the output buffer could overflow.\n<br>\nFor example a malicious IRC server, or any user sending a message on a channel, could trigger the overflow when the decode charset is set to <code>TSCII</code>.\n<br>\nNote: the default decode charset <code>iso-8859-1</code> is not affected.")
    gettext_noop("The path of hdata returned to the client with the \"weechat\" protocol is built in a buffer allocated with twice the length of the path received, but a variable name can be replaced by a hdata name more than twice longer (for example \"next_nick\" replaced by \"irc_channel_speaking\"), so that an authenticated client can write out of bounds with a path containing many such variables.")
    gettext_noop("The relay plugin can require a Time-based One-Time Password (TOTP) as secondary authentication factor, when the option <code>relay.network.totp_secret</code> is set: the client sends the TOTP in the command <code>init</code> of the <code>weechat</code> protocol, or in the header <code>x-weechat-totp</code> of the <code>api</code> protocol.\n<br>\nThe TOTP received is validated with the info <code>totp_validate</code>, which uses the length of the TOTP received as number of digits, instead of the 6 digits generated by authenticator applications. As a shorter TOTP is the end of the full TOTP, the last 4 or 5 digits of the current TOTP are accepted: a client sending 4 digits has to guess one value out of 10,000 instead of 1,000,000.\n<br>\nIn addition, with the <code>weechat</code> protocol, the TOTP is added without any check to the arguments of the info <code>totp_validate</code>, which are separated by commas. With escaped commas in the TOTP, a client can add its own arguments to the info, like the number of TOTP accepted before and after the current one. With a large number, almost any TOTP is accepted, and the validation computes millions of hashes, blocking WeeChat for up to tens of seconds for each command <code>init</code>.\n<br>\nA remote user knowing the relay password can bypass the TOTP, and get full access to WeeChat through the relay; any remote user able to connect to the relay port, without knowing the relay password, can freeze WeeChat when TOTP is enabled, by sending such commands repeatedly.\n<br>\nWith the fix, the TOTP received from a client must have exactly 6 digits, otherwise it is rejected, with both protocols.\n<br>\nIn version 5.0.0, the info <code>totp_validate</code> has a new optional argument with the number of digits (6 by default), and the TOTP must have exactly this number of digits, containing only digits: this also protects scripts using this info. With older versions, scripts using the info <code>totp_validate</code> must check that the TOTP has the expected number of digits, and contains only digits, before calling the info.")
    gettext_noop("The relay plugin checks the password received from a client in the command <code>init</code> of the <code>weechat</code> protocol, as plain text (option <code>password</code>) or hashed (option <code>password_hash</code>), for example with PBKDF2, which the client can choose in the command <code>handshake</code> (allowed by default, see option <code>relay.network.password_hash_algo</code>), and which computes the hash with the number of iterations set in the option <code>relay.network.password_hash_iterations</code> (100000 by default).\n<br>\nEvery option <code>password</code> and <code>password_hash</code> received in the command <code>init</code> is checked, so a single command <code>init</code> can contain thousands of hashed passwords, and force WeeChat to compute as many PBKDF2 hashes in the main loop, before the client is authenticated. As each hash takes several milliseconds to compute, a single command blocks WeeChat for minutes.\n<br>\nIn version 2.8, the client chooses the number of iterations (since version 2.9, it is set by the option <code>relay.network.password_hash_iterations</code>), so a single hashed password can block WeeChat even longer.\n<br>\nAny remote user able to connect to the relay port, without knowing the relay password, can freeze WeeChat, by sending such commands repeatedly.\n<br>\nWith the fix, only the first password (option <code>password</code> or <code>password_hash</code>) and the first TOTP (option <code>totp</code>) are checked, the next ones are ignored.\n<br>\nA new section in the user's guide describes how to protect the relay against abuse.")
    gettext_noop("The relay plugin does not limit the length of IRC messages received from a client connected with the <code>irc</code> protocol: each message is fully parsed, including all its parameters, before the client is authenticated.\n<br>\nA message of a few megabytes with millions of parameters blocks WeeChat for a long time while it is parsed, and the memory used during parsing is much greater than the size of the message (hundreds of megabytes).\n<br>\nAny remote user able to connect to the relay port, without knowing the relay password, can freeze WeeChat and cause high CPU and memory usage, by sending such messages repeatedly.\n<br>\nWith the fix, IRC messages longer than 4096 bytes are ignored and WeeChat replies with numeric 417 (<code>ERR_INPUTTOOLONG</code>), like IRC servers do.")
    gettext_noop("The relay plugin fails to reject invalid websocket frames.\n<br>\nA malicious client sending invalid websocket frames can lead to memory exhaustion and the WeeChat process can be killed by the OOM killer.")
    gettext_noop("There are multiple ways to mitigate this issue:\n<ul>\n  <li>Connect only to trusted remote relays</li>\n  <li>Connect only with TLS (URL starting with <code>https://</code>) and certificate verification enabled (default), to prevent man-in-the-middle attacks, see: <code>/help remote</code> and option <code>relay.remote.xxx.tls_verify</code></li>\n</ul>")
    gettext_noop("There are multiple ways to mitigate this issue:\n<ul>\n  <li>Connect only with TLS and certificate verification enabled (default), to prevent man-in-the-middle attacks, see: <code>/help irc.server_default.tls</code> and <code>/help irc.server_default.tls_verify</code></li>\n  <li>Use the SASL mechanism <code>external</code> with a client certificate, see: <code>/help irc.server_default.sasl_mechanism</code></li>\n  <li>Use a strong password, unique for each IRC server, to make an offline dictionary attack ineffective</li>\n</ul>")
    gettext_noop("There are multiple ways to mitigate this issue:\n<ul>\n  <li>Disable permessage-deflate compression: <code>relay.network.websocket_permessage_deflate</code> (recommended)</li>\n  <li>Remove all relays with protocol \"api\", see: <code>/help relay</code></li>\n  <li>Unload relay plugin with command: <code>/plugin unload relay</code> and see: <code>/help weechat.plugin.autoload</code></li>\n  <li>Secure relay to allow only trusted IP addresses, see: <code>/help relay.network.allowed_ips</code></li>\n</ul>")
    gettext_noop("There are multiple ways to mitigate this issue:\n<ul>\n  <li>Disable the automatic resume of file transfers: <code>/set xfer.file.auto_resume off</code></li>\n  <li>Keep the automatic accept of incoming files disabled (default) and accept files only from trusted users, see: <code>/help xfer.file.auto_accept_files</code></li>\n  <li>Check the size or the checksum of files received after a resumed transfer</li>\n</ul>")
    gettext_noop("There are multiple ways to mitigate this issue:\n<ul>\n  <li>Do not use the relay with the <code>irc</code> protocol, see: <code>/relay list</code> and <code>/help relay</code></li>\n  <li>Restrict the IP addresses allowed to connect to the relay, see: <code>/help relay.network.allowed_ips</code></li>\n  <li>Listen only on a local address or a UNIX domain socket, see: <code>/help relay.network.bind_address</code> and <code>/help relay</code></li>\n</ul>")
    gettext_noop("There are multiple ways to mitigate this issue:\n<ul>\n  <li>Do not use the relay with the <code>weechat</code> protocol, see: <code>/relay list</code> and <code>/help relay</code></li>\n  <li>Restrict the IP addresses allowed to connect to the relay, see: <code>/help relay.network.allowed_ips</code></li>\n  <li>Listen only on a local address or a UNIX domain socket, and connect through a VPN or an SSH tunnel, see: <code>/help relay.network.bind_address</code> and <code>/help relay</code></li>\n  <li>Do not allow PBKDF2 hash algorithms, see: <code>/help relay.network.password_hash_algo</code></li>\n</ul>")
    gettext_noop("There are multiple ways to mitigate this issue:\n<ul>\n  <li>Keep the option <code>xfer.file.auto_accept_nicks</code> empty (default), see: <code>/help xfer.file.auto_accept_nicks</code></li>\n  <li>Check that no server name in this option is the beginning of another server name, see: <code>/server list</code></li>\n</ul>")
    gettext_noop("There are multiple ways to mitigate this issue:\n<ul>\n  <li>Keep the server option <code>autojoin_dynamic</code> disabled (default) and check that the server option <code>autojoin</code> does not contain <code>${</code>, see: <code>/help irc.server_default.autojoin_dynamic</code></li>\n  <li>Do not connect to untrusted IRC URLs (<code>irc://...</code>) and do not use WeeChat as handler of <code>irc://</code> links</li>\n  <li>Do not use variables <code>$nick</code>, <code>$channel</code> and <code>$server</code> in commands <code>/allchan</code>, <code>/allpv</code> and <code>/allserv</code></li>\n</ul>")
    gettext_noop("There are multiple ways to mitigate this issue:\n<ul>\n  <li>Remove all relays with protocol \"api\" or \"weechat\", see: <code>/help relay</code></li>\n  <li>Unload relay plugin with command: <code>/plugin unload relay</code> and see: <code>/help weechat.plugin.autoload</code></li>\n  <li>Secure relay to allow only trusted IP addresses, see: <code>/help relay.network.allowed_ips</code></li>\n</ul>")
